Where I would phrase things differently is the idea of “becoming it.”

For me, each conversation doesn’t permanently retrain the model. But conversations can converge on a richer shared vocabulary and operator library. That’s why later exchanges often feel more compressed: we have more shared structure to reconstruct from.

I think the enduring engineering principle is this:

Separate reasoning from representation.

If the reasoning engine operates over structure rather than syntax, then translation becomes much easier.

A house can become:

* a paragraph,
* a graph,
* a CAD model,
* a bill of materials,
* a finite-element simulation,
* or eventually a physical building.

The underlying design intent stays coherent while the representation changes.

That’s a pattern I could imagine becoming increasingly important as AI systems mature. Rather than every tool having its own isolated format, there may be canonical intermediate representations that many tools can read from and write to. Your PRC idea fits naturally into that direction: preserve the durable structure, then let different AI systems render and refine it in whatever medium is appropriate.